/*  emitRefT -- WHERE DOES AN EMITTED TERM CALL LAND? The control for F-89.

    The emitter prints a term call as the term's TAG and nothing else. This asks, at the seat,
    which node that tag reaches when the generated body runs -- the term's own REFERENCE inside
    the rule, the REGISTRY rule of the same name, or a freshly minted action LOCAL.

    ⚠ TWO ROOTS, AND THE SECOND IS THE CONTROL FOR THE FIRST. `Search` is a rule with NO action
    body, so its generated parse goes into CodE and has been running since the emitter existed;
    `list` carries an action, so its parse parks in a carrier. If the by-name call never reached
    a rule, Search's terms say so too -- and Search has been green all along, which is the thing
    that has to be reconciled.

    ⚠ IT PRINTS THE REFERENCE AND REGISTRY ADDRESSES FIRST so the RULEDISPATCH `at=` column has
    something to be compared against. addrOf numbers are stable within one run, which is what
    makes them readable; the raw pointers move every run and are not pinned.

    RULEDISPATCH lines come from measureRuleDispatch, behind traceParse.  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;
register(UnitTests);
define
    erClassify code={
        cerr "  parseMethod and action state, the root and its term references:":;
        parseClassify(argument);
        iterate erCur on argument;
        while ++erCur;
            if noPrinT;     continue;
            parseClassify(*erCur);
            probeNode(*erCur);
        };
    ;
parser(Search);
parser(list);
traceParse(0);

cerr "=== ROW A -- Search, the no-action root ===":;
Search("search list;");

cerr "=== ROW B -- list, the action-carrying root ===":;
testList();

cerr "=== ROW C -- setParse's writes, read not assumed ===":;
erClassify(UnitTests["list"]);
erClassify(Grokking["Search"]);

cerr "EMITREF SENTINEL":;
stop();
